WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Time for the mental model that explains almost every “why did it do that” moment for the rest of the series: what actually happens when you send a single turn.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:06.686
Here's the loop.

3
00:00:06.686 --> 00:00:10.286
The app assembles a prompt and sends it, along with the list of tools, to the model.

4
00:00:10.286 --> 00:00:12.429
The model replies with either text or a tool call.

5
00:00:12.429 --> 00:00:15.686
If it's a tool call, the app runs it, adds the result, and goes round again.

6
00:00:15.686 --> 00:00:16.929
If it's text, the turn stops.

7
00:00:16.929 --> 00:00:18.000
That's the entire engine.

8
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.378
The consequence that surprises people: every trip around that loop is a separate, separately-billed request to the model.

9
00:00:22.378 --> 00:00:24.586
A task that makes eight tool calls is nine requests, not one.

10
00:00:24.586 --> 00:00:29.000
That's why an agentic task costs far more than a single question — and why it's worth being specific so it doesn't wander.

11
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.779
What actually goes into that prompt, in order?

12
00:00:30.779 --> 00:00:40.177
Global instructions, then project instructions, then this chat's instructions, then any recalled memory, then retrieved knowledge, then the skill index, then the tool definitions, then the conversation history — and your new message goes last.

13
00:00:40.177 --> 00:00:43.000
Knowing this order explains a lot about what the model pays attention to.

14
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.376
And why does the loop ever stop?

15
00:00:44.376 --> 00:00:50.828
Five reasons: it answered you; it hit the step limit; it caught itself repeating unproductively; you stopped it; a spend cap kicked in; or it errored.

16
00:00:50.828 --> 00:00:55.000
If a task ends earlier than you expected, it's one of these — usually the step limit or a repeat.

17
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.460
One gotcha that saves hours of confusion.

18
00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.739
Those instruction layers add up — they don't replace each other.

19
00:00:58.739 --> 00:01:01.445
A chat prompt does not override the global one; they're all present at once.

20
00:01:01.445 --> 00:01:04.543
So the commonest cause of an ignored instruction is a contradiction between two layers.

21
00:01:04.543 --> 00:01:07.000
When something's ignored, look for what's fighting it, not for a bug.

22
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.233
Now you know how a turn works.

23
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:13.000
Next we make the privacy picture concrete: exactly what runs on your machine versus what leaves it for the provider.

